.TH INVOQUE 1 "2026-08-13" "scheduler" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
invoque \- launch an agent session against one project, preloaded with its status report
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invoque
\fIproject\fR
.br
.B invoque
\fIproject\fR \fB\-\-summary\fR
.br
.B invoque
\fIproject\fR \fB\-\-interactive\fR|\fB\-I\fR
.br
.B invoque
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invoque
hands one project's offline status report to a live agent session, on
demand, for a caller sitting at a prompt right now. It is the single-shot
counterpart to
.BR dose (1),
which dispatches the same class of session on a
recurring, unattended schedule against the shared paced quota \- the two
divide "run an agent" the way
.BR jauge (1)
divides "measure spend" from
"cause it": neither verb does the other's job.
.TP
.I project
(bare, no mode)
Print the same offline status report
.B scheduler status
.I project
already produces \- git health, pending feedback, open questions, the
project's last automated run. Reads only; reaches no paid service.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Run a read\-only one\-shot pass over that report and the project's own
tree, and print a short summary. Spends on every call.
.TP
.BR \-\-interactive ", " \-I
Replace this process with a live, interactive session in the project's
own directory, preloaded with the report as its first message. Spends on
every call.
.SS It wraps, it does not reimplement
Both spending modes exec the identical code path
.BR "scheduler status" 's
own
.B cmd_status
has run since before this verb existed \- same report builder, same
session launch, unchanged. The bare report is fetched first in every
invocation, free, so a mistyped project name is caught before a spending
mode is ever reached.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Not wired for this verb. Requesting it is reported rather than ignored.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The bare report was printed. (A spending mode that completes and returns
control exits with whatever the session itself exited \- see the note
below, not this code.)
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: no project given, an unrecognised extra argument, or the
project named does not resolve.
.TP
.B 4
GAP:
.I bin/scheduler
is absent or not executable under the resolved checkout, so no report
can be built and nothing was spent.
.PP
Once a spending mode's session actually starts, this process
.B becomes
that session \- the same way
.BR ssh (1)
becomes the remote command it launches. Its exit code is whatever the
session itself exits with, which is not part of this contract: a session
that ran and disagreed with what it found is not this verb failing.
.SH EXAMPLES
The bare form is free and offline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ invoque realisateur
scheduler status: realisateur -- 2026-08-13 16:32

\-\- git health (/home/scheduler/Documents/Projects/realisateur) \-\-
clean, up to date with origin
...
$ echo $?
0
.fi
.RE
.PP
A project that does not resolve is a usage error, not a gap, and nothing
is spent to discover it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ invoque does\-not\-exist
invoque: scheduler: no such project 'does\-not\-exist', or it has no PROJECT_REPO_PATH set
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
No project at all:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ invoque
invoque: usage: invoque <project> [\-\-summary|\-\-interactive|\-I]
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH COST
This verb spends money: every
.B \-\-summary
or
.B \-\-interactive
call reaches a paid model session, by design \- that is the entire
reason it exists, not an escalation from a missing mechanism. It carries
no
.B \-\-summon
flag: that flag is for a verb whose stated job is something else,
declaring it can escalate to an agent only for a gap not yet mechanized.
Here there is no such gap to name \- launching a session
.B is
the job \- so the cost boundary is the two mode flags themselves, which
the caller must always name explicitly. The bare form never spends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/invoque
This utility.
.TP
.I bin/scheduler
The legacy implementation it wraps; carried on a host with a scheduler
checkout, GAPs honestly on one without.
.TP
.I lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime it sources.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dose (1),
.BR jauge (1),
.BR rapporte (1),
.BR relis (1),
.BR arme (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Expectations transfer from
.BR dose (1)
for the domain this verb was cut out of, and from
.BR ssh (1)
for what happens to the exit code once a spending mode replaces this
process with a live session.
.SH HISTORY
Coined 2026-08-13 with
.B bashify coin
as the sixth verb of one noun, in answer to hf7y/scheduler#109: no
existing verb could gain "launch an agent with a prompt template and
instructions" without its NAME line needing "and", so this is new rather
than an extension.
.SH GAPS
"Specific instructions" beyond the fixed status\-report template \-
freeform text a caller appends to what a spending mode preloads \- is not
yet built. Recorded in
.I GAPS.md
rather than silently omitted; the two modes above cover exactly what the
legacy status command they wrap already covered before this page existed,
unchanged.
